<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Sylvester II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 946–1003</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Gerbert of Aurillac, Sylvester II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Sylvester II, born Gerbert of Aurillac, was a French scholar who became the first French pope, reigning from 999 to 1003. As a young monk he studied in Catalonia, at Vic and probably Ripoll, where he learned mathematics and astronomy drawn from Arabic sources, and he later taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. He promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abacus">abacus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-arabic-numeral-system">Hindu-Arabic numerals</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and worked closely with Emperor Otto III.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Sylvester II" width="320" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meister der Reichenauer Schule · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meister_der_Reichenauer_Schule_002_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gerbert of Aurillac</b>, the future Pope Sylvester II, studied in Catalonia in the 960s, where mathematics and astronomy from al-Andalus were beginning to circulate in Latin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He was crowned king at Christmas 1000 or on 1 January 1001, with a crown that, according to his later legends, was sent by <b>Pope Sylvester II</b> with the approval of Emperor Otto III.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By traditional reckoning Grand Prince Stephen was crowned with a crown sent by <b>Pope Sylvester II</b>, making Hungary a Christian kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its church centres flourished: Ripoll's library made it a hub of learning in the 10th century, where <b>Gerbert of Aurillac</b> studied Arabic science.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-osona">County of Osona</a></li><li><blockquote>Géza's son Vajk, baptised Stephen, defeated the pagan Koppány and was crowned king on Christmas Day 1000 (or 1 January 1001) with a crown from <b>Pope Sylvester II</b>, founding the Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301).</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hungary">Principality of Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
